  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "serial_blosc",
    "file": "c-blosc2/blosc/blosc2.c",
    "description": "`serial_blosc` writes each block-start entry with `_sw32(bstarts + j, ntbytes)` without first verifying that the 4-byte entry fits in `context->destsize`. The extended header is 32 bytes, but `write_compression_header` places `bstarts` immediately after it and reserves another 4 bytes per block logically; a destination of only `srcsize + BLOSC2_MAX_OVERHEAD` can be shorter than that logical reservation for very small inputs. The write therefore occurs before compression can fail or fall back to memcpy. In this PoV, a 34-byte allocation receives a 4-byte write at offset 32, overflowing by 2 bytes.",
    "conditions": "The harness derives a 2-byte chunk from PoC `[!`, allocates `nbytes + BLOSC2_MAX_OVERHEAD` = 34 bytes in `blosc2_schunk_append_buffer`, and passes that as the compression destination. Byte 0 selects compression level 1 (so this is not the memcpy path); the 2-byte source yields one block. The extended header consumes 32 bytes, `bstarts` points at destination offset 32, and `output_bytes` is initialized to 36. With compression active, non-memcpy, and no dictionary training, `serial_blosc` enters line 1991 and writes the one 4-byte block offset into bytes 32..35 although only bytes 0..33 are allocated. Generally this is triggered when `header_overhead + 4*nblocks > destsize` on the regular compression path; the code must reject/fall back before writing the block-start table or bounds-check each entry."
  },
